# Fibonacci Divisibility: From Growth to Synchronization

The Fibonacci sequence is famous for growth, but its indices also carry an exact divisibility geometry. Begin with the computational overview below, then explore each structural layer interactively.

{{demo:0}}

## 1. Compress a family through its index gcd

The identity $\gcd(F_a,F_b)=F_{\gcd(a,b)}$ extends to every finite family. Instead of constructing many large Fibonacci numbers, compute one small index gcd and one Fibonacci value.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite transport proof</summary>

Use induction on the finite family. The empty family gives $0=F_0$. Inserting $a$ changes the value gcd to $\gcd(F_a,F_g)=F_{\gcd(a,g)}$, where $g$ was the previous index gcd. This is precisely the gcd of the enlarged index family.

For background, see [greatest common divisors](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor) and [Fibonacci numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence).
</details>

## 2. Watch a primitive prime become a clock

At the prime index $q=13$, the prime $p=233$ first appears in $F_{13}$. It then divides $F_m$ exactly at multiples of $13$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact apparition argument</summary>

If $p$ divides both $F_q$ and $F_m$, then it divides $F_{\gcd(q,m)}$. Since $q$ is prime, the gcd is $1$ or $q$. It cannot be $1$ because $F_1=1$. Hence $q$ divides $m$. Conversely, $q\mid m$ implies $F_q\mid F_m$.
</details>

## 3. Synchronize an arbitrary finite family

A single prime now tests a whole collection. The prime $233$ divides the gcd of the selected Fibonacci values exactly when $13$ divides the gcd of their indices.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite-family theorem</summary>

Divisibility by $233$ of the family gcd is equivalent to divisibility of every family member. Exact apparition translates this to divisibility of every index by $13$, which is equivalent to $13$ dividing the index gcd. The empty family is included because both finite gcds are $0$.
</details>

## 4. Certifying new prime factors

For composite indices in the certified interval, inherited prime factors can be stripped using Fibonacci values at proper divisors. A remainder larger than $1$ contains a genuinely primitive prime.

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why stripping proper divisors is sufficient</summary>

If a factor of $F_n$ had appeared at an earlier index $k<n$, strong divisibility would force it into $F_{\gcd(n,k)}$. That gcd is a proper divisor of $n$. Therefore a factor coprime to all Fibonacci values at proper divisor indices cannot have appeared earlier.
</details>

## 5. Continue exploring

The same architecture suggests extensions to [Lucas sequences](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas_sequence), rank-of-apparition lcm laws, and [elliptic divisibility sequences](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_divisibility_sequence). The central reusable pattern is simple: preserve gcds, locate a primitive divisor, and lift one-index information to every finite family.

